  chapterSnapshot: 'NCERT Class 6 Science (Curiosity) Chapter 3: Mindful Eating: A Path to a Healthy Body (QR: 0677CH03). A comprehensive, scientifically grounded exploration of food components, biochemical testing, nutritional physiology, and sustainable eating habits. Rooted in ancient wisdom ("Kodaruk? Hitabhuk, Mitabhuk, Rtubhuk" and Taittiriya Upanishad\'s "annena jātāni jīvanti"), the chapter bridges traditional agro-climatic diets across Punjab, Karnataka, and Manipur with modern biochemical principles. It investigates the primary food macronutrients: Energy-giving Carbohydrates (sugars, starch in cereals and tubers) and Fats (concentrated energy stores and animal insulation); Body-building Proteins for growth and cellular repair; Protective Nutrients (Vitamins A, B1, C, D and Minerals Calcium, Iodine, Iron); Dietary Fibre (Roughage) for bowel regularity; and Water. The chapter provides exact laboratory protocols for biochemical assays: Starch testing with dilute iodine (blue-black complex), Fat detection via translucent paper patches, and Protein confirmation using the Biuret assay (Copper Sulfate + Caustic Soda yielding violet). It maps vitamin and mineral deficiencies to specific clinical conditions (Night blindness, Beriberi, Scurvy, Rickets, Goitre, Anaemia) and details landmark historical breakthroughs including James Lind\'s 1746 discovery of citrus cures for scurvy and salt iodisation in the Himalayan goitre belt. It contrasts the empty-calorie profile of ultra-processed junk food (potato wafers) with nutrient-dense traditional foods (roasted chana), highlights the pioneering legacy of Dr. Coluthur Gopalan (Father of Nutrition Research in India, founder of PM POSHAN mid-day meals), champions climate-resilient native Nutri-cereals (Millets: Jowar, Bajra, Ragi, Sanwa), and teaches sustainability through the calculation and reduction of Food Miles.',
